# Untitled

Adsorption-Desorption Induced Structural Changes of Cu-MOF Evidenced by Solid State NMR and EPR Spectroscopy
Department of Chemistry and Applied Bioscience, ETH Z¨urich, Ho¨nggerberg, HCI, 8093 Z¨urich, Switzerland, and Institute of Chemical Technology, UniVersity of Stuttgart, 70550 Stuttgart, Germany
Porous metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are promising materials for gas storage, separation, and catalysis.1 To some extent, their unique properties can be attributed to their structural flexibility, which affords them a predominant capability to response to external stimuli.2 Recently, intensive efforts have been made to demonstrate that the robust MOFs act as sponges upon evacuation/inclusion of physisorbed guests, whereas their crystallinity still remains.3 As promising catalysts in green chemistry, MOFs possess properties of both homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysts so that they can exhibit high catalytic performance and also easily be separated from reactants and products. Solid-catalyzed heterogeneous reactions generally are initiated by adsorption of reactants on the active sites, followed by surface reaction and desorption steps. The active sites are regenerated upon desorption of products from the surface. A crucial question to be addressed is whether the above surface processes can affect the structure of the MOF materials. To the best of our knowledge, such chemisorption-desorption induced local structural changes of MOFs have not been evidenced so far, and understanding of such phenomena may be crucial for properly assessing the potential of MOFs for technical applications.
Chemisorption of probe molecules in porous MOFs, which results in formation of chemical bond or change of interaction strength between guest molecules and host framework, is considered as a direct approach to study their structural flexibility. High resolution MAS NMR spectroscopy is very sensitive for the local structure of adsorbates and surface sites and provides more detailed separation and assignment of signals,4 while continuous-wave (CW) EPR is well suited for characterizing the coordination environment of transition metal sites.5 In this communication, for the first time, MAS NMR combined with EPR spectroscopy gives direct experimental evidence for the flexible structure of MOFs during adsorption-desorption of probe molecules.
The two-dimensional grid $\mathrm{Cu(bpy)(H_{2}O)_{2}(B F_{4})_{2}(b p y)}$ [Cu-MOF] $(\mathrm{bpy}=4{,}4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) sheets comprise hydrogen-bonded bpy and bpy coordinated at equatorial positions of a Jahn-Teller elongated $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ coordination octahedron. This provides some flexibility in bond cleavage or inclusion.6a The $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOF exhibits volume expansion $(6.66\%)$ during $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption at a pressure of 101 kPa.7c In this work, $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOF was selected as a model to study the structural flexibility induced by adsorption-desorption of probe molecules.
The $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOF was prepared according to the literature6b and vacuumed at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ under a pressure below $10^{-2}$ mbar for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ (see Supporting Information, SI). As shown in Figure 1a, boron atoms in the $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOF caused a narrow signal at ca. $-2.0\ \mathrm{ppm}$ , which corresponds to tetrahedral coordination. In comparison with free $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ anions,4b the polarizing effect of $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ species on the $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ anions is responsible for the decrease of the nuclear shielding resulting in a weak low-field shift of the signal. Upon adsorption of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ on the $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOF (ca. $2.0\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ per $\mathrm{Cu}$ (II) atom), the ${}^{11}\mathrm{B}$ NMR signal was shifted to $-4.9\mathrm{ppm}$ corresponding to a highfield shift of $\Delta\delta_{11\mathrm{B}}=-2.9$ ppm (see Figure 1b). It indicates that the interaction of the $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ species and fluorine in tetrafluoroborate became weaker due to chemisorption of $\mathrm{CH_{3}O H}$ on these active sites, which shifts the ${}^{11}\mathrm{B}$ MAS NMR signal to a higher magnetic field. Interestingly, when this sample was degassed (0.1 mbar, rt, $20~\mathrm{min},$ ) to partially remove the adsorbed $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , the ${}^{11}\mathrm{B}$ signal in Figure 1c was reversibly shifted back to the same resonance position $(\delta_{118}=-2.0\ \mathrm{ppm})$ as in the original material shown in Figure 1a. It was reported that MOFs exhibited structural flexibility in gas storage under high pressure.3,7 Here, the structural change in Cu-MOF was obviously detected even upon loading/removing of a few mbar probe molecules.
After loading of ca. $0.5\mathrm{\CH_{3}C N}$ per $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ atom on the CuMOF (Figure 2a), a new signal appeared at $-5.8$ ppm corresponding to $\Delta\delta_{118}=-3.8~\mathrm{ppm}$ , which was accompanied by a decrease of the ${}^{11}\mathbf{B}$ signal at $-2.0\ \mathrm{ppm}$ (Figure 2b). Upon loading of ca. 2.0 $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ per $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ atom, the signal at $-2.0$ ppm totally moved to $-5.8~\mathrm{ppm}$ , indicating that virtually all $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ sites interacted with $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ . This finding clearly shows that the structural flexibility is directly caused by chemisorption at $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ sites: the more $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ sites interact with probe molecules, the more neighboring boron atoms are involved in the high-field shift in the NMR spectra. Additional evidence is given by the correlation between the electric dipole moments of the probe molecules and the structural flexibility of the MOF. The higher electric dipole moment of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ led to a stronger interaction with the $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ sites, which made $\mathrm{{Cu-F}}$ bonds much weaker and resulted in a larger high-field shift of the ${}^{11}\mathrm{B}$ MAS NMR signal $(\Delta\delta_{118}~=~-3.8~\mathrm{ppm}$ ) compared with that observed upon $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ adsorption $(\Delta\delta_{118}=-2.9\mathrm{\ppm})$ ). After adsorption of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ the sample was vacuumed at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ ; the $\mathbf{\tau}^{11}\mathbf{B}$ MAS NMR signal in Figure 2d was reversibly shifted back to $-2.0$ ppm.
To verify binding of the probe molecules and reversible structural changes upon their adsorption, CW X-band EPR spectra of the $\mathrm{Cu}.$ - MOF loaded with $\mathrm{CH_{3}O H}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ were recorded. No narrowing of the lines or hyperfine splitting was observed upon cooling from room temperature to $120~\mathrm{K}$ . The spectrum of the $\mathrm{Cu}$ -MOF after being vacuumed at $373\mathrm{~K~}$ is typical for a six-coordinated $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ complex with an elongated coordination octahedron (Figure 3a). The copper hyperfine interaction is averaged by exchange between neighboring centers.8 This exchange narrowing does not average the $g$ tensor, as the molecular frames of all copper centers coincide in the crystal structure.6,7 Axial coordination of $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ is consistent with $g_{||}=g_{z}=2.314$ , and a slight splitting of the $g_{\perp}$ feature is due to the asymmetry of equatorial bpy coordination.
EPR spectra taken after adsorption of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ or $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ on the $\mathrm{cu}$ -MOF exhibit pronounced shifts of $g_{||}$ (Figure 3b and 3c), thus indicating coordination changes at all axial sites. These changes in the spectra are accompanied by a color change from violet to lavender blue. Slight sharpening of the $g_{\perp}$ feature is observed in the high field part of the EPR spectrum. This can be traced back to a closer approach to axial symmetry, i.e., a smaller difference between $g_{x}$ and $g_{y}$ (see SI).
Interestingly, when both samples were exposed to air, their EPR spectra (see Figures 3d and S3) were reversibly changed back to the original state. Both the change of $g_{||}$ and the change in the small orthorhombic splitting in the high field part are reversible. This indicates that the $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ anions again dominantly interacted with $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ at the axial positions. This is consistent with the NMR spectra obtained after vacuum (Figures $1-2$ ) or exposure to air (see Figure S5). Cu-MOF loaded with $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , which has a lower electric dipole moment, regained the original structure much faster upon exposure to air than that loaded with $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ . Upon air exposure, the $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOF reverted almost completely to the structure of the original material, demonstrating that the humidity of laboratory air was not sufficient for water adsorption in the copper centers. After strong condensation of water a new EPR spectrum with $g_{||}{=}2.259$ is observed (see Figure S4).
In summary, the current work presents direct experimental evidence of MOF structural flexibility during adsorption-desorption, which are important steps in gas storage, separation, and catalysis. Exposure of the Cu-MOF to a few mbar of probe molecules $\mathrm{\CH_{3}O H}$ , $\mathrm{CH_{3}C N})$ ) already induced structural changes. The EPR spectra show that these molecules are activated to form complexes at $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ sites, and a change of the Cu-MOF’s structure occurs as indicated by a high-field shift of the ${}^{11}\mathrm{B}$ MAS NMR signal. After desorption, both EPR and ${}^{11}\mathrm{B}$ MAS NMR results show that the Cu-MOF structure reversibly shifted to the original state. This observation indicates that MOFs can undergo structural changes already under relatively mild conditions. Understanding of these dynamic changes is inevitable for a rational design of MOFs for technical applications.
